{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And you shall not allow the salt of the covenant of your Elohim to be lacking</b> — that the covenant was cut for salt from the six days of Genesis, when the lower waters were promised to be offered upon the altar in salt and in the water libation on the festival — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 2:13). And it is a midrash of the Sages. And R. Abraham Ibn Ezra said (Ibn Ezra on Leviticus 2:13), according to the plain sense: 'I have brought you into a covenant and I have adjured you not to offer unsalted, and it shall not be eaten, for that is a way of disgrace.' And because it is a covenant in the offerings, Scripture makes this covenant the prototype for all covenants, and speaks similarly concerning the gifts of the priesthood (Numbers 18:19) and concerning the kingdom of David (II Chronicles 13:5): 'a covenant of salt,' for it is as enduring as the covenant of salt in the offerings. But R. Abraham explained there that 'covenant' means 'cut,' derived from 'a salt land' (Jeremiah 17:6), and the place of salt is cut off. And there is no sense to his words. And because it says here 'the covenant of your Elohim' and does not say 'the covenant of YHWH' in the language of the portion and the manner of all the offerings, or say 'the covenant of YHWH your Elohim,' I understand in it the concept that salt — water and the power of the sun that comes upon them produce salt, and the waters in their generative nature irrigate the earth and give birth and bring forth growth, but after they become salt they destroy every place and burn it, so that it is neither sown nor sprouts. And behold, the covenant encompasses all the attributes, and water and fire enter into it, and it is measured out toward it, and there comes into it the first governance, the kingdom of HaShem, like salt that gives flavor to all foods, and it sustains and destroys through its saltness — and behold, salt is like the covenant. Therefore Scripture said (II Chronicles 13:5): 'Do you not know that YHWH, the Elohim of Israel, gave the kingdom to David over Israel forever, to him and to his sons, a covenant of salt forever,' for this too is the attribute of David; and therefore it said concerning the offerings (Numbers ibid.): 'It is a covenant of salt forever,' for the covenant of salt — through it the eternal is established and cut. And I have already instructed you (Exodus 31:13) to contemplate from our words in other places the meaning of these three terms: 'It is an everlasting covenant.'"], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra is cited first by his epithet 'R. Abraham' and then referred to again as 'R. Abraham'; both refer to the same person, Abraham Ibn Ezra. The phrase 'and it is measured out toward it' (ועדיה תאתה) is textually difficult and rendered hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:50:01.286098875Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}